|term[reference][referent][expected-cardinality]
  :status proposed
  :word reference
  :avoid [pointer link address]  ; each names a narrower kind, defined later or not at all
  :depends []

  A **REFERENCE** is recorded material that stands for something beyond itself: its **REFERENTS**, the things it is intended to determine. Standing-for is the primitive notion here, and the two words simply name its ends: the reference is the material doing the standing-for; the referents are the things stood for. Neither gets an independent definition, because neither has one — take away the relation and nothing is left of either word. We deliberately define the reference as including what are elsewhere parts of a "reference act" — the *intent* — with the part of the intent tractable to the theory being its **EXPECTED-CARDINALITY**: when the reference is first created, what does its creator intend for it to find? A single referent ({1,1})? At most one ({0,1})? As many things as there are in a place ({0,N})?

  |invariants
    - A reference is an artifact: it persists, travels, and is copied; the mind that writes it and the mind that uses it may be far apart in time, place, and kind
    - It works in its referents' absence — that is its purpose: acting on what is not at hand
    - Determination is deferred: a reference is written once and used later, possibly many times, and nothing guarantees the world holds still in between; that gap is constitutive, not incidental
    - The expected cardinality belongs to the reference (declared at writing), never to whatever later processes it, and it is what gives a miss its meaning: zero against {1,1} says the writer's model of the world is wrong; three against {1,1} says the reference narrowed insufficiently; zero against {0,N} is simply the answer
  |examples [a mailing address, a wikilink, an import statement, a URL, "the Joseph in accounting", a citation key]

  |working-notes
    - Defining reference and referent as co-defined poles is deliberate, not a shortcut: any "independent" definition of either smuggles the other in (the thing referred to; the material that refers). How the relation is made effective — how material actually reaches things — is exactly the next distinction (@[binding], @[match]); this entry only establishes that the relation exists, is directional, and is carried by an artifact.
    - The word deliberately shares udon core's "reference" (the @-selector): the core's construct is the special case, this term the generalization, and sharing the word says so.
    - Philosophy-of-language note: strictly (Strawson), agents refer using expressions — referring is an act. The artifact-first cut is chosen because the theory's machinery manipulates artifacts (documents carry references; stores resolve them); the definition absorbs the act's theory-tractable part (the creator's intent, as expected cardinality) into the artifact, and the events are named with plain verbs — a reference is written, then used. A prior draft made the act the headword (REFERENCE-ACT); retired, since no event-noun has yet been needed.
    - The postal on-ramp in one line: an envelope's address block is a reference; each line strips candidates; the intended cardinality is one recipient; the days between writing and delivery are the gap in its plainest form.
    - Expected-cardinality settled early on convergence: SQL's scalar subquery (a {1,1} with only a hard error channel), hybrid logic's nominals ({1,1} in the semantics), wikilink stem lookup, steward O15.
    - Deliberately left open: the operational-vs-epistemic double bound; @{TARGET-SET} vs referent seating; the internal composite structure of references (real references are usually conjunctions of parts — the address's several lines; treated when paths are); a reference's ORIGIN — resolution is origin-relative, the reference itself is not (see @[resolution]).
